    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper investigates the impact of climate sentiment on U.S. sector-level and industry-level stock returns within the S&P 500 index from 1985 to 2022. Leveraging advanced natural language processing techniques derived from transformer architecture, I construct a Climate Change Concerns Index (CCCI) and conduct sentiment analysis on a comprehensive newspaper corpus of 3 million articles. Employing fixed effects regression models while controlling for sector and time fixed effects, I find robust evidence of a significant association between climate sentiment and stock returns. Negative climate sentiment is associated with a substantial reduction in returns across almost all S&P 500 sectors and industries, with a 1-point increase in climate sentiment corresponding to a 0.16% decrease in stock returns on average. Notably, sectors identified as climate-vulnerable by authoritative climate Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) texts demonstrate heightened sensitivity to pessimistic climate sentiment, experiencing more pronounced negative effects on U.S. stock returns compared to non-specified industries. This research underscores the relevance of climate-adjacent factors in investment decision-making.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
